---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import AskParkinsons from "../components/AskParkinsons.astro";
import {
  approvedCopy,
  contactEmail,
  definition,
  headline,
  secondaryNavigation,
  siteName,
} from "../content/site";
---

<SiteLayout title="Evidence, experience, and exploration — review preview" wide>
  <section id="introduction" class="page-section hero" data-section="P1">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Meridian Station · Early-stage initiative</p>
        <h1>
          {headline.slice(0, headline.indexOf("evidence"))}<span
            >{headline.slice(headline.indexOf("evidence"))}</span
          >
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-definition">{definition}</p>
        <p class="initiative-label">
          <strong>Parkinson's: our first initiative.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="medical-boundary">
          This is an informational and collaboration project, not a clinical
          service. We do not diagnose or provide treatment advice.
        </p>
        <div class="intro-actions">
          <a class="section-action" href="#parkinsons-initiative"
            >Explore the Parkinson's initiative</a
          >
          <a class="secondary-action" href="#our-approach">Our approach</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-panel">
        <svg
          class="direction-art"
          viewBox="0 0 320 210"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <path class="trajectory-back" d="M25 175 C105 175 70 40 180 40 H280"
          ></path>
          <path class="trajectory-front" d="M25 145 C100 145 95 75 180 75 H245"
          ></path>
          <path class="trajectory-tip" d="M253 13 L280 40 L253 67"></path>
          <path class="trajectory-warm" d="M25 195 H75"></path>
        </svg>
        <p class="eyebrow">Our direction</p>
        <h2>Research.<br />Understanding.<br />Better questions.</h2>
        <p>
          Our first major goal is an AI-assisted Parkinson's knowledge resource:
          source-linked research explained for patients and families preparing
          questions for healthcare professionals. We are also exploring tools
          for understanding observations over time. This technology direction is
          under development, not an available research platform.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <nav class="section-map" aria-labelledby="section-map-heading">
      <h2 id="section-map-heading">On this page</h2>
      <ul>
        {
          secondaryNavigation.map(({ label, href }) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href}>{label}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </section>

  <AskParkinsons />

  <section id="why-change" class="page-section section-split" data-section="P2">
    <h2>Why look at change over time?</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>
        A research finding or an isolated observation can leave practical
        questions unanswered. What was measured, under what conditions, and what
        happened before or after? A snapshot can miss context that matters to
        the question.
      </p>
      <p>
        We want to make research easier to explore alongside questions about
        everyday change, without treating personal observations as equivalent
        evidence. Collecting more information is not the goal in itself: its
        relevance, quality, missing context, and burden matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        For us, <strong>longitudinal</strong> means paying attention to change over
        time rather than treating each observation as an isolated snapshot. It does
        not mean assuming that one event caused another.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="our-approach"
    class="page-section approach-section"
    data-section="P3"
  >
    <h2>Evidence. Experience. Exploration.</h2>
    <p>
      Three connected ideas guide the project. Keeping their differences visible
      matters as much as exploring them together.
    </p>
    <div class="concept-grid">
      <div class="concept-module" data-concept="Evidence">
        <h3>Evidence</h3>
        <p class="concept-question">
          <strong
            >What does published research report, and how strong is its support?</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          Evidence means published research, how it was studied, the sources
          supporting a claim, and its limitations. We intend to make claims
          traceable. A citation is a starting point for checking a claim, not a
          reason to stop asking questions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-module" data-concept="Experience">
        <h3>Experience</h3>
        <p class="concept-question">
          <strong>What does a person notice over time?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Experience means what a person observes or records over time, with
          context and limitations. It helps shape the questions worth asking. We
          will not treat one person's observation as proof of a treatment effect
          or a cause.
        </p>
        <p>
          That does not dismiss lived experience. When personal reports are used
          in research, we will ask how they were collected and what the study
          can reasonably conclude.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-module" data-concept="Exploration">
        <h3>Exploration</h3>
        <p class="concept-question">
          <strong>What could emerging technology help us investigate?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Exploration means questions, prototypes, methods, and technologies
          being investigated, not validation. AI, sensing, and other tools are
          not answers in themselves. We intend to judge experiments by what they
          can demonstrate and to state what remains unknown.
        </p>
        <p>
          An experimental tool is not established medical treatment. People
          remain responsible for interpretation and decisions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="direction-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Research Assistance</h3>
        <p>
          The intended direction is AI-assisted research discovery and
          explanation, with source-linked outputs, citations, provenance,
          limitations, and uncertainty visible. Comparing findings should help
          people ask better questions, not delegate medical answers to an
          autonomous system.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Longitudinal Understanding</h3>
        <p>
          We are exploring how observations over time could be understood with
          their context, missingness, provenance, and measurement limitations.
          Patterns would be questions to investigate, not proof of causation.
          Any future collection requires a justified purpose and separate
          safeguards.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Initiatives</h3>
        <p>
          Parkinson's is the first application of this direction. The mission is
          broader, but there is no promise to expand to other conditions or to
          build three separate products.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="parkinsons-initiative"
    class="page-section initiative-section"
    data-section="P4"
  >
    <h2>Parkinson's: our first initiative.</h2>
    <p>
      Parkinson's is where this work begins. It is personally meaningful to the
      founder and gives the initiative a focused place to start asking questions
      about research, experience, and change over time.
    </p>
    <div class="initiative-grid">
      <div class="initiative-feature" data-feature="knowledge">
        <p class="eyebrow">Research assistance</p>
        <h3>AI-assisted knowledge resource</h3>
        <p>
          The first major goal is to develop an AI-assisted Parkinson's
          knowledge resource that can organize credible research, preserve
          source citations and limitations, and explain complex information in
          language patients and families can use when learning about the disease
          and preparing questions for healthcare professionals.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="initiative-feature" data-feature="longitudinal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Experience over time</p>
        <h3>Longitudinal understanding</h3>
        <p>
          A second area of exploration is longitudinal information:
          understanding whether responsibly collected observations such as
          movement, exercise, sleep, symptoms, environmental conditions, and
          other measurements can help people recognize patterns and formulate
          better questions over time. Any future collection would need a defined
          purpose, consent and privacy safeguards, and evaluation of measurement
          limitations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      These tools are being explored as educational and research technologies.
      They are not intended to diagnose Parkinson's, prescribe treatment, or
      replace medical care.
    </p>
    <p>
      Parkinson's is the first initiative, not the limit of the organization's
      interests. Any future expansion would need its own purpose and
      justification.
    </p>
    <p>This website is not enrolling a study or collecting health records.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="origin" class="page-section origin-section" data-section="P5">
    <h2>How the question began</h2>
    <div class="origin-grid">
      <div class="origin-transition">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it started</p>
        <p class="origin-name">Ride for Parkinson's</p>
        <p>Awareness + Technology</p>
        <span class="origin-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        <p class="origin-name">Meridian Station</p>
        <p>A broader mission. The same curiosity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="origin-story">
        <p>
          An early software-career experience at Rebar Foundry, a dot-com-era
          incubator exploring new technologies, helped shape the founder's
          interest in turning emerging ideas into practical experiments.
        </p>
        <p>
          After decades building software, Parkinson's and cycling brought new
          questions into that work. Ride for Parkinson's began as an awareness
          effort and became a catalyst for real-world technology
          experimentation: live-tracking prototypes, mobile-development
          questions, and how to make sense of observations over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ride's wider mission included an AI-assisted knowledge resource
          for patients and families. It prompted a broader question: how could
          published evidence, experience over time, and emerging technology help
          people understand health questions without confusing their different
          roles?
        </p>
        <p>
          {siteName} grew from that connection, not from a claim that ride observations
          proved a treatment effect. One person's experience is a starting point for
          questions, not a substitute for other perspectives or scientific evidence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="current-work"
    class="page-section work-section"
    data-section="P6"
  >
    <h2>What exists today</h2>
    <div class="work-list">
      <article class="work-item">
        <div>
          <h3>Earlier ride work</h3>
          <p class="status-label">Status: Prototype — prior ride artifacts</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            The Ride for Parkinson's source includes a route map, a
            browser-based phone tracker, and Firebase-backed public tracking
            code. Its records discuss native mobile development and a future
            educational AI resource. Source artifacts establish prior
            prototyping, not a verified operating tracker, working AI system,
            completed ride, or formal research study.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="work-item">
        <div>
          <h3>Current website and research definition</h3>
          <p class="status-label">Status: Prototype — static review website</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            This implemented website explains the initiative and invites project
            collaboration. The approved product brief, requirements, and earlier
            written prototype support current research-definition work; they do
            not constitute an available knowledge resource or personal-data
            platform. Audience review remains part of refining the explanation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="work-item">
        <div>
          <h3>Technology direction under development</h3>
          <p class="status-label">
            Status: Active development — direction and scope
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>{approvedCopy.futureCapabilities}</p>
          <p class="documentation-date">
            Status evidence checked: <strong
              ><time datetime="2026-10-29">29 October 2026</time></strong
            >, by the founder's direction and local source review. This is not
            operational or clinical verification, or the final public content
            review date.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="work-item">
        <div>
          <h3>Future capabilities need separate implementation</h3>
          <p class="status-label">
            Status: Future exploration — separate implementation
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            Any future work involving personal records, sensing, or analysis
            would need separate requirements, privacy safeguards, and
            evaluation. Public research information and private personal
            information would remain separate. No collection or analysis of
            visitor health information is implemented here.
          </p>
          <p>
            This first website will not offer accounts, health logging, device
            connections, AI chat, or personalized medical interpretation.
          </p>
          <p><a href="#collaborate">Help shape the next step</a></p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="collaborate"
    class="page-section collaboration-band"
    data-section="P7"
  >
    <div class="collaboration-intro">
      <h2>Help shape the next questions</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          You do not need to be a researcher or developer to offer a useful
          perspective.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="contact-panel">
      <p>Ways to contribute could include:</p>
      <ul class="contribution-examples">
        <li>
          Offering lived-experience perspectives on the questions and language,
          without sharing personal health histories.
        </li>
        <li>
          Challenging an assumption or suggesting a more careful research
          approach.
        </li>
        <li>Helping with design, accessibility, and clearer information.</li>
        <li>
          Discussing research assistance or a bounded technical investigation.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are starting points for a conversation, not staffed openings or
        promises of a project role.
      </p>
      <div class="contact-warning">
        <h3>Before you email</h3>
        <p>
          Please keep your message about the project. <strong
            >Do not send medical records, symptom histories, medication details,
            location traces, or other sensitive health information. Do not
            attach files.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          This channel does not provide medical advice and is not monitored for
          urgent help. Contacting us does not enroll you in research or
          authorize publication of your message.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p id="contact-privacy">
        Email shares your address and message with the email services involved.
        Read <a href="/privacy/">Privacy and contact information</a> before deciding
        what to send.
      </p>
      <h3>Email about the project</h3>
      <p class="contact-address">
        <a class="email-address section-action" href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}
          >{contactEmail}</a
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        You can use your own email service. If an email application does not
        open, copy the address into a new message.
      </p>
      <div class="message-guidance">
        <p>
          A short note about the question or contribution you have in mind is
          enough. You do not need to disclose a diagnosis or personal health
          history. Your name, organization, and professional background are
          optional.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can use the same address to flag a correction or ask about
          information handling.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="contact-expectation">{approvedCopy.contactExpectation}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="sources"
    class="page-section section-split sources-section"
    data-section="P8"
  >
    <h2>Sources and context</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>
        When we make a research claim, we intend to show where it comes from and
        explain the limits that matter to its interpretation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Personal accounts and project proposals are identified as such. They are
        not presented as scientific findings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
